- [x] MPEG-TS multiplexer, live ingest and egress
- [x] Live preview thumbnails, in-client playback, tiles removed when a stream stops
- [x] Multi-node shape: StatefulSet, headless DNS, per-pod ingest services (k8s/live/)
- [ ] SRT needs an FFmpeg built with libsrt; point Media__LibraryPath at one
- [ ] Local UDP ingest is blocked by this machine's inbound filtering (see README)
- [x] Own the SRT listener: libsrt directly, deleting the accept carousel and the log-line capture

Scaling to a thousand streams. The plan, the reasoning and the research behind each of these is in
`.scratch/scale-to-1000/plan.md`; the phase numbers below are its.

- [x] Verify Phase 1 and 2 on Windows: 194 passed, 0 failed, 8 skipped (Postgres only). The
      resolver found srt.dll beside the FFmpeg natives, which is the branch Linux never takes
- [x] Stress the snapshot race fix: 100 runs under constrained CPU, zero failures, against a rate
      of roughly one in eight before it
- [x] Phase 2: one SRT latency option applied to both listeners, and a one-second GOP in the
      reference senders. The join-latency floor is the encoder's keyframe interval
- [x] Phase 1b: a live name is locked. A second publisher of a name that is live and held is
      refused at the handshake, rather than taking it over
- [x] Verify cross-pod behaviour on k3s. Found two defects, both fixed: a graceful shutdown was
      worse for a viewer than a crash, and a stream that moved replicas got a new start time
- [ ] Decide who sweeps abandoned registry entries. A force-killed pod leaks one per stream into
      Redis forever, and until something sweeps them a graceful shutdown cannot interrupt instead
      of delete, which is what a rolling update needs to keep a stream's start time
- [x] Refresh docs/replica-failover.md and the README's SRT section. Both described the accept
      carousel, the log-line capture and the self-test as load-bearing; all three are deleted
- [x] Phase 0 baseline on one container: see `.scratch/scale-to-1000/baseline.md`. Accept rate is
      at least 20/s and the rig was always the limit, not the service
- [x] Phase 3: the in-cluster viewer hop becomes HTTP, deleting the SRT relay and AvioStream
- [x] Raise k8s/live/deployment.yaml's resources. Was 200m/512Mi with a 1Gi limit, which OOM-kills
      at about 20 camera-rate streams; now 2 cores and 3Gi with a 4Gi limit, and the arithmetic is
      in the manifest
- [x] Multi-port ingest via Live__IngestPortCount. The threads do multiply, but the gain is about
      2x rather than 4x and costs a third more CPU and memory per stream: 17 pods becomes 8-9
- [ ] Re-measure multi-port on a real node. The load generator ran out of machine before the
      service did, so the ratio is "about two" rather than a number
- [ ] Measure whether pooling StreamDemuxer.Pump's per-packet byte[] moves the per-stream thread
      cost. Two threads per stream at ~1.7% of a core is what actually dominates, not the preview
- [x] gRPC live RPCs guarded by the same token as REST, via an interceptor. The client must send it
      on ListLive too, not only on the three actions
- [x] KLV extraction: an always-attached packet subscriber on the SMPTE KLV index, decoding the ST
      0902 minimum set from ST 0601 with ST 1402 carriage detected, the ST 0102 classification on
      LiveStream and on snapshot and recording metadata, and GET /api/live/klv/{name}
- [x] Phase 11, second half: has_klv, last_klv_at and the marking on ListLive (fields 16-18, 19-25
      held), and GetLiveKlv forwarding to the owner. The client can now be built entirely on gRPC
- [x] Phase 11, first half: health on ListLive, the preview proxying to the owner, retention on
      GetProviders, and the token guard. Fields 14-15, 4-5 The client stays gRPC
      by decision; health, marking, a proxied preview and KLV must reach ListLive and friends
- [x] MISB ST 0903 encoder: the VMTI wire format detections leave as, round-tripped through a
      reader written from the standard. Nothing produces one yet
- [x] Decided: VMTI rides standalone as its own KLV stream. Never burned into the picture, never
      nested in ST 0601 tag 74, so recorded imagery is byte-for-byte what the encoder sent
- [x] Detection provenance: a capture carries the detection that caused it, on both surfaces, and
      documents are findable by detection. An alert is that document appearing on the change feed
- [ ] Flaky under a full-suite run: A_reconnect_under_the_same_name_resumes_the_same_stream. Passed
      3/3 alone, 16/16 in its class and on a repeat full run; the SRT timing tests share ports and
      ffmpeg senders with the rest of the suite
- [x] Export what the service already measures: OTLP to a collector, Prometheus and Tempo behind it,
      and a Grafana dashboard each for streaming and for the API. No /metrics endpoint on the pod -
      the .NET Prometheus exporter is still beta and this service has public ports. The meters
      grew with it: the census a beat publishes, what each stream fed, claims and endings, viewer
      attaches by route, overflows, capture outcomes, the heartbeat's own latency, and on the API
      side the open server streams, document bytes by surface and the hop between replicas.
      docs/observability.md says what to alert on
- [x] A high-load end-to-end test, and the meters checked rather than assumed: fifty encoders on one
      ingest port, viewers opening and closing, a snapshot and a recording on every stream at once,
      ten feeds killed with an audience and without, and every published figure held against what the
      replica was actually doing - the owned gauge against the encoders started, recorded bytes
      against the documents, hub bytes against the senders' own payload, and no tag carrying a name,
      which fifty names can show and one cannot. Clean on four cores at 50 x 0.8 Mbit/s: 98 per cent
      delivered, no kernel receive errors, a 1 ms median beat. LIVE_LOAD_STREAMS uses it as the rig
- [x] Find the edge, on four cores, with a rig that is not its own bottleneck: LiveScaleTests ramps
      ingest to 200 streams and readers to 500 and names what ran out, per thread, out of /proc.
      Answers in .scratch/scale-to-1000/ingest-and-readers.md. 200 streams and 550 readers is not
      where it breaks - 148 Mbit/s in, 437 out, 1.4 of 4 cores, nothing lost. Camera-rate ingest is:
      clean to 100 streams, losing 6.8 percent of packets at 150 while delivering 116 percent of the
      rate through retransmits, collapsed at 200. Readers are nearly free - 2000 of them cost 0.6 of
      a core and ingest never noticed
- [ ] (#4) A slow relayed viewer holds a thread, and nothing bounds how many. Measured: 200 readers pausing
      three seconds a read took the pool from 12 workers to 219, one each, while not one subscriber
      overflowed. Serve writes through PacketMuxer synchronously because libav has no async write
      callback, so a consumer that will not take the bytes holds the thread writing to them. Viewers
      per pod are therefore bounded by threads rather than bandwidth, on the route a cluster uses for
      every viewer that reached the wrong pod. Bounded writers with a dropping queue, an async write
      path, or disconnect a viewer that cannot keep up - any of the three beats a thread. #19 took the
      async write path on the relayed route and left this scoped to the direct one; #11 then measured
      that route and found no per-viewer thread to save there for an ordinary player, because libsrt
      discards from the send buffer rather than making the send wait. The thread is back only in the
      case #20 now carries: a viewer that cleared its own packet-drop flag
- [x] (#5) ViewerQueuePackets is 2000 packets, which at 25 fps is eighty seconds of lag before skip-to-live
      engages, and a viewer eighty seconds behind live is not a viewer. RecorderQueuePackets has the
      same shape at 20000. Both want to be seconds of media rather than packets, since the packet is a
      frame and the frame rate is the encoder's choice
      Viewer half done (#19): ViewerQueueSeconds, with ViewerQueuePackets as the ceiling. Recorder half
      closed as measured and not worth it (#21): a recorder's queue can only be floored, never capped,
      by the packet figure - overflowing it destroys the document rather than costing a skip - and with
      that floor the seconds change nothing at or below ~67 packets/s, which is every stream this
      service carries. A 25 fps camera lands back on 20000 exactly; +10% with AAC, +30% for KLV, 3x
      only above ~197 packets/s. What actually made the recorder's queue matter was that it had to
      absorb a whole part upload, and taking that off the read path (#21) is what fixed it. Revisit if
      a >200 packets/s transport ever arrives. What the packet figure still is not is a memory bound:
      20000 packets of a 25 Mbps feed at 25 fps is ~2.5GB against a 4Gi pod, because a packet gets
      bigger as the rate falls. That wants stating in bytes, like BufferByteCeiling, and is its own job
- [ ] (#6) Nothing bounds concurrent snapshot decodes: LibavMediaAnalyzer.OnAFileAsync spills every request
      to a temp file and runs libav in Task.Run with no valve, so 200 snapshots at once are 200 temp
      files and 200 decodes on the same pool as everything else. About 15 ms each here, so it passes at
      200 and will not at a thousand with a detector triggering captures. A semaphore the width of the
      processor count costs nothing and removes the queue
- [x] (#7) Withdrawn: there is no document tail worth the name, and the 18 s this entry used to claim was
      my own measurement error. The stopwatch started when the rig got round to asking rather than when
      the recorders stopped, so at 200 streams it was timing thirty seconds of a forty-five-second
      recording and reporting three quarters of it as a queue of document writes. Re-anchored on the
      live.recordings.active gauge reaching zero, the last document lands 0.1 s after the final
      recorder stops - measured again at 200 streams recording 400 s each while #21's decoupling rolled
      a part under all of them. Storage write plus document write are still serialized by LiteDB on
      this configuration, so the shape of the worry was right; the number was not
- [x] (#11) Measured a slow player on the consumption port - ffmpeg -readrate 0.3 against a 12.29 Mbit/s
      stream, ten of them, two minutes - in LiveSlowPlayerTests, with the mechanism measured on the
      sending socket in SrtSendPressureTests rather than inferred from it. For a player whose handshake
      advertised too-late-packet drop, which is libsrt's default and what an ordinary player does, the
      send does not wait: against a peer that had stopped reading, libsrt held 1581 KB at 1020 ms of
      the 12000 KB the buffer offers, discarded 461 packets a second out of it, and left no send
      outstanding at any sample. Ten slow players then skipped nothing to live, which bounds every
      single write below the four-second queue without counting a thread, and cost fewer than half a
      thread each - so one thread each, #4's relayed shape, is excluded; the test cannot tell that
      apart from none, since the peak of 8 workers and 33 threads against 6 and 31 lands during the
      attach. The player loses picture, which
      is what #11 predicted: a reader taking three tenths is handed 1.9 Mbit/s of a 12.29 Mbit/s stream
      and loses about 980 packets a second once its own buffer is full, and one pacing strictly by
      bytes is disconnected by its own library after about thirty seconds ("SEQUENCE DISCREPANCY ...
      Reception no longer possible"). Ingest, the healthy player and the publisher's own link were
      untouched throughout. What a slow viewer costs in memory is NOT settled here: resident grew
      4.6 MB per player in the run of record and between 5.8 and 20.7 in earlier ones, and that figure
      carries a muxer, a subscription and a four-second queue as well as the 1.5 MB of send buffer, so
      the split is inference and the rig knob (LIVE_SCALE_SLOW_DIRECT) exists to measure the slope
      properly. .scratch/scale-to-1000/slow-direct-players.md is the run
- [ ] (#20) No SRTO_SNDTIMEO is set anywhere, and the block that leaves is reachable by any caller.
      Measured in SrtSendPressureTests: with the peer's too-late-packet-drop flag cleared, nothing
      frees the sending socket's buffer, it filled to its twelve megabytes in fourteen seconds and one
      send was still inside libsrt twelve seconds later, returning only when the socket was closed
      under it. A caller clears that flag with one query parameter (tlpktdrop=0), or raises the drop
      threshold instead by asking for a longer latency, since the handshake settles at the larger of
      the two sides; the consumption port admits every caller by design, and since #18 the thread that
      waits is a pool worker, so a handful of them starves the body that disposes newly accepted
      sockets. Set a send timeout on accepted viewer sockets and decide what a timeout means - skip
      forward or drop the viewer - together with SRTO_SNDBUF and ViewerQueueSeconds, because the three
      are one policy. The coupling is the trap: any per-viewer send buffer below bitrate x 1.02 s,
      which is 1.5 MB at 12.3 Mbit/s, guarantees the buffer reaches its ceiling before the drop
      threshold can free it, and so manufactures this block for every viewer rather than only for the
      one that asked for it
- [ ] (#13) The scale rig exercises no detection, no KLV and no forwards. Detection is the one that
      would move the ceiling: the frame tier decodes at keyframes for the preview, and a detector asks
      for a rate, which makes the decoder decode everything between keyframes for those streams
- [ ] (#14) LibavMediaAnalyzerTests.RunAsync and GrpcApiTests.SampleJpegAsync start the bundled ffmpeg
      without LD_LIBRARY_PATH, where SrtSenders sets it, so those five tests fail on a Linux machine
      carrying a fetched shared build in ffmpeg/linux-x64 - which is how a Linux developer gets SRT -
      while every SRT test in the same run passes
- [ ] (#8) Pool StreamDemuxer.Pump's per-packet byte[]. Now measured rather than suspected: the per-stream
      demux threads are 1.8 cores at 200 camera-rate streams against the receive worker's 0.24, so
      ingest per pod is bounded by demultiplexing cost per packet and this is the thread to make
      cheaper. It supersedes the preview as the thing to look at first
- [ ] (#10) baseline.md's receive-worker budget (streams/175 + pps/45000 < 1, knee at 60 percent of that
      thread) does not hold outside the rig it was measured on. On a container over loopback the same
      thread never passed 24 percent, at any load, including through a collapse, so the formula
      over-predicts by an order of magnitude and must not size a Linux node. Decide whether to
      re-measure it per environment or delete it and size from loss and kernel drops instead
- [ ] (#9) Decide what a viewer's thread should be. Task.Factory.StartNew with an async delegate spends the
      LongRunning thread at the first await, so 300 concurrent viewers cost no threads at all - good
      at this scale - while one OS thread is still created and thrown away per accepted viewer, which
      is waste during exactly the reconnect storm that produces them. Either Task.Run and a comment
      that says so, or a genuinely synchronous serve loop
- [ ] (#12) Find the reader ceiling with a socket in it. The 2000-reader figure is in-process HTTP against
      the test server: it measures the subscription, the muxer and the copy, and no socket, no
      Kestrel write path and no SRT egress. Whatever binds readers is in the part that rig replaces
- [ ] Export the detection worker's meter too. It already publishes live.detection.duration on the
      same meter name, and nothing carries it off the pod: the worker is a separate deployment with
      no exporter wired, so today it is readable only with dotnet-counters against that process
- [ ] Decide whether logs should ride OTLP beside the metrics and traces. Serilog writes structured
      JSON to stdout and the cluster collects it; one sink would let Grafana pivot from a span to
      the lines inside it, at the cost of a second path for something that already works
- [ ] Detection, per .scratch/scale-to-1000/detection-plan.md: geometry first, then RF-DETR on the
      processor, the worker, the GPU, YOLO, and tracking
- [ ] Decide the Ultralytics licence question before YOLO ships in anything delivered. RF-DETR's
      smaller variants are Apache-2.0 and clean; Ultralytics is AGPL-3.0 or commercial
- [ ] Phase 8: the ingest pod stops decoding; a worker tier decodes once per stream on a GPU and
      returns detections and the preview. Detection runs on every stream, so decoding on demand
      answers nothing; KLV needs no decode at all and must not argue for any
- [x] Validate the KLV decoder against a real MISB stream. Esri's FMV sample: 711 packets, every
      checksum verified, and the slant range agrees with the geometry from four independently
      scaled encodings to under two metres. No scale was wrong
- [ ] Benchmark RF-DETR on the target GPU before sizing the worker tier: frames per second per GPU
      at a useful batch size, and whether NVDEC or the model saturates first
- [ ] Separate detection rate from tracking rate. Pattern of life needs stable identities, which is
      a tracker property; detecting every frame on every stream is the most expensive option there is
- [x] The overload signal, proven on the rig at 50, 100 and 250 streams: per-stream loss and drop
      name the degraded streams, the pod-level kernel counter is the collapse itself, and both
      agree with delivered bitrate. Phase 4 is unblocked; decide over a window of beats
- [x] Phase 4: Live__MaxStreams refuses at the handshake when full, always admitting a name the pod
      already owns, with the autoscaling and alerting guidance in the manifest
- [ ] Give a pod permission to write its own pod-deletion-cost, if scale-down ever moves enough
      encoders to hurt. No Role or RoleBinding exists anywhere under k8s/ today
- [ ] Phase 5: optional SRT passphrase, enforced during the handshake
- [ ] Phase 6: pulled streams survive their pod, claimed by the heartbeat under the existing lock
- [ ] Lengthen SrtSocketStream's receive timeout, now that srt_close is proven to unblock a read
- [x] Rewrite the README's "About SRT" section and CONTEXT.md's "Claim"
- [x] Drop the README's link to docs/design/server-side-stream-ingest.md, which was never in the
      repo. CONTEXT.md, the drawio and replica-failover.md carry what it would have said
- [x] Synthesised ST 0601 for static cameras, carried in band on a synthetic track: a fixed camera
      configured with a position, a pointing and a field of view is indistinguishable, to every
      consumer, from one that reports its own telemetry. The seam is shared with VMTI, which is not
      in this change. Per-viewer opt-in is a non-goal: a KLVA track on stream type 0x06 is what
      every real FMV stream carries and players ignore it
